<chapter xml:id="ch-data-shapes" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Data Shapes</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-data-shapes">
    <p>
      <xref ref="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data" /> worked because
      every dataset in it was already arranged the way <c>ggplot2</c>
      expects: one row per observation, one column per variable. Add a
      variable, add an aesthetic mapping. That trick only works if the data
      is already in the right <term>shape</term> for it. Real data rarely
      arrives that way, and the same numbers, rearranged, can be either
      trivial or nearly unusable depending on how they are laid out. This
      chapter is about recognising that shape is a real, consequential
      property of a dataset, not a formatting detail.
    </p>
  </introduction>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  SECTION 0 (new lead story, added after sec-jhu-shapes /            -->
<!--  sec-shapes-ebola-callback / sec-shapes-applied were already        -->
<!--  written and reviewed - per the user, those three sections are      -->
<!--  UNCHANGED below, not moved, not reworded). Real Kenyan case study  -->
<!--  on maternal mortality data, verified against three real sources    -->
<!--  before writing:                                                    -->
<!--    - WHO (2022). Maternal mortality measurement: guidance to        -->
<!--      improve national reporting. Geneva: World Health Organization. -->
<!--      Verbatim quotes re-checked via a second, quote-specific fetch. -->
<!--    - Smith, H., Ameh, C., Godia, P., Maua, J., Bartilol, K.,        -->
<!--      Amoth, P., Mathai, M. and van den Broek, N. (2017).            -->
<!--      "Implementing Maternal Death Surveillance and Response in      -->
<!--      Kenya: Incremental Progress and Lessons Learned." Global       -->
<!--      Health: Science and Practice, 5(3), 345-354.                  -->
<!--      DOI 10.9745/GHSP-D-17-00130. Full text supplied directly by    -->
<!--      the user; journal/volume/pages confirmed via independent       -->
<!--      search since the user's excerpt didn't name the journal.       -->
<!--    - Ahmed, S.M.A., Cresswell, J.A. and Say, L. (2023).             -->
<!--      "Incompleteness and misclassification of maternal death        -->
<!--      recording: a systematic review and meta-analysis." BMC        -->
<!--      Pregnancy and Childbirth, 23, 794. DOI                        -->
<!--      10.1186/s12884-023-06077-4. Volume/article number confirmed   -->
<!--      via independent search.                                       -->
<!--  Framing device (the three "maternal_death = TRUE" datasets that    -->
<!--  look joinable) is this book's own pedagogical illustration, not    -->
<!--  something drawn from any of the three sources - kept distinct     -->
<!--  from the sourced facts around it, same practice as elsewhere in   -->
<!--  this book (e.g. the Berkeley/Ebola insight framings).             -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <section xml:id="sec-maternal-mortality-shapes">
    <title>How Many Mothers Died?</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In 2014, Kenya was strengthening its Maternal Death Surveillance
        and Response (MDSR) system, an effort to identify every maternal
        death, investigate its causes, and act on what was found. The
        question the system needed to answer sounds simple: <em>how many
        women died because of pregnancy and childbirth that year?</em>
      </p>

      <p>
        Kenya had several systems that could, in principle, answer it. Health
        facilities reported deaths through the District Health Information
        System (DHIS). MDSR teams notified and investigated individual
        deaths. Civil registration recorded and classified deaths. Large
        household surveys estimated maternal mortality a different way
        again. Every one of these systems produced a number. The numbers did
        not agree.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mmr-numbers-disagree">
      <title>Two numbers, both defensible, both far apart</title>

      <p>
        The national estimate for 2014 put the number of maternal deaths in
        Kenya at around 8,000, based on an estimated maternal mortality
        ratio of 510 per 100,000 live births. The DHIS, drawing on reports
        from health facilities, recorded 945 maternal deaths that year.
      </p>

      <p>
        Neither number was wrong. The DHIS was capturing deaths that
        occurred in, or were reported through, health facilities. The
        national estimate was attempting to capture maternal deaths across
        the whole population, including deaths that never touched a health
        facility at all. Two systems, asked what sounds like the same
        question, were actually measuring different things <mdash /> and the
        eight-fold gap between 945 and 8,000 is what that difference looks
        like once it reaches a spreadsheet.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mmr-five-systems">
      <title>Five systems, five different questions</title>

      <p>
        Kenya's data landscape for maternal mortality was not two sources
        but at least five, and each one was built to observe something
        distinct.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-mmr-sources">
        <title>What each Kenyan maternal mortality data source actually observes</title>
        <tabular halign="left">
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Source</cell>
            <cell>What is being observed</cell>
          </row>
          <row><cell>DHIS</cell><cell>Maternal deaths reported through health facilities</cell></row>
          <row><cell>MDSR</cell><cell>Maternal deaths notified and investigated through the surveillance system</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Civil registration</cell><cell>Registered deaths and their classified causes</cell></row>
          <row><cell>DHS (household survey)</cell><cell>Deaths reported retrospectively by surviving sisters</cell></row>
          <row><cell>National estimate</cell><cell>A modelled estimate incorporating available sources</cell></row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        These are not five copies of one dataset with different error rates.
        The household survey approach, for instance, asks a respondent
        whether her sister died during pregnancy, delivery, or the
        postpartum period <mdash /> an estimate that depends entirely on what
        that respondent remembers and is willing to report, years later, about
        someone else's death. A facility system depends on a death being
        recorded at all, by a facility, while it was happening. Civil
        registration depends on a death certificate being completed and
        correctly classified. Each method has a different relationship to
        the truth it is trying to capture, before any question of data
        quality even arises.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mmr-same-name">
      <title>Same column name, different definition</title>

      <p>
        Imagine a statistician receives three datasets to combine: one from
        DHIS, one from civil registration, one from a household survey. Each
        has a column called <c>maternal_death</c>, and in each, some rows
        are marked <c>TRUE</c>. The datasets look wonderfully compatible.
        Join them, and the merge runs without a single error.
      </p>

      <p>
        But what does <c>TRUE</c> actually mean in each dataset? In one, it
        means a facility recorded and reported the death. In another, it
        means a death certificate was completed and classified as maternal.
        In the third, it means a survey respondent recalled, possibly years
        later, that a sister had died around the time of a pregnancy. The
        join succeeds mechanically. It does not make the three
        <c>TRUE</c>s the same claim.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-mmr-variable-name">
        <title>A variable name is not a definition</title>
        <p>
          Two datasets can both contain a column called
          <c>maternal_death</c>, and the column can be produced by
          completely different processes, with different coverage,
          different timing, and different failure modes. Nothing about a
          successful join checks this. R does not know, and cannot know,
          whether two columns with the same name mean the same thing; it
          only knows whether the rows line up. Before combining data from
          different sources, the question is not just <q>do the rows
          match?</q> It is <q>are the things being combined actually
          comparable?</q> <mdash /> and that question has to be answered by
          understanding where each dataset came from, not by inspecting the
          merged table afterward.
        </p>
      </insight>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mmr-kenya-findings">
      <title>What Kenya's own implementation found</title>

      <p>
        Smith et al. (2017), reporting directly on Kenya's MDSR
        implementation, describe exactly this gap turning into an
        operational problem. In 2014, only about 12% of the estimated
        annual number of maternal deaths were notified through the DHIS2
        system at all. Of the 945 deaths that DHIS did record that year,
        only 484 (51.2%) were ever formally assessed. Of those 484, 447
        (92.4%) were found to have involved suboptimal care <mdash /> but
        that finding applies only to the roughly one in twenty maternal
        deaths that were estimated to have occurred <em>and</em> were
        captured by a facility <em>and</em> were subsequently reviewed.
      </p>

      <p>
        The paper is explicit about why: under-reporting of maternal deaths;
        difficulty reviewing the data that was reported; suboptimal
        aggregation of cause-of-death information; and a persistent gap
        between DHIS and the Civil Registration and Vital Statistics system.
        Even after MDSR notification and review forms were integrated
        directly into DHIS, the paper reports, <q>the system has gaps, and
        the DHIS and Civil Registration and Vital Statistics system do not
        yet adequately capture all maternal deaths in Kenya.</q> Integrating
        the forms fixed a technical merging problem. It did not fix the
        underlying definitional and coverage gaps between what each system
        was built to see.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mmr-not-unique-to-kenya">
      <title>Not unique to Kenya</title>

      <p>
        This is not a story about one country's data systems falling short.
        WHO's own guidance on maternal mortality measurement calls
        specifically for <q>robust collection of valid, reliable and
        standardized data,</q> for <q>improved quality and completeness of
        various data sources and systems, and linkages between them,</q>
        and for <q>appropriate integration, interpretation and use of
        data.</q> Guidance like this exists precisely because the gap
        between what different systems observe is a structural feature of
        maternal mortality measurement everywhere, not a defect specific to
        any one country's implementation.
      </p>

      <p>
        A 2023 systematic review and meta-analysis by Ahmed, Cresswell and
        Say puts a number on how large that gap typically is. Across the
        studies they reviewed, maternal death recording was incomplete by an
        estimated 34% on average, with substantial variation between
        settings, and maternal mortality overall was underestimated by an
        estimated 39% once incompleteness and misclassification were both
        accounted for. The Kenyan case is not an outlier. It is a
        particularly well-documented instance of a gap that shows up almost
        everywhere maternal deaths are counted.
      </p>

      <p>
        Read this way, <q>data shapes</q> is not only about rows and
        columns. It is about what an observation and a variable actually
        represent <mdash /> and two datasets that look identical in
        structure can still be measuring entirely different things.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Sources:</term> World Health Organization (2022).
        <em>Maternal mortality measurement: guidance to improve national
        reporting.</em> Geneva: WHO. Smith, H., Ameh, C., Godia, P., Maua,
        J., Bartilol, K., Amoth, P., Mathai, M. and van den Broek, N.
        (2017). <q>Implementing Maternal Death Surveillance and Response in
        Kenya: Incremental Progress and Lessons Learned.</q> <em>Global
        Health: Science and Practice</em>, 5(3), 345<ndash />354. Ahmed,
        S.M.A., Cresswell, J.A. and Say, L. (2023). <q>Incompleteness and
        misclassification of maternal death recording: a systematic review
        and meta-analysis.</q> <em>BMC Pregnancy and Childbirth</em>, 23, 794.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  SECTION 1: JHU CSSE COVID-19 dashboard                           -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <section xml:id="sec-jhu-shapes">
    <title>A Format That Grew a New Column Every Day</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In January 2020, a small team at Johns Hopkins University's Center
        for Systems Science and Engineering (CSSE) launched a public COVID-19
        dashboard, described the following May in a peer-reviewed paper by
        Dong, Du and Gardner (2020). It became, within weeks, one of the
        single most-used sources of COVID-19 data in the world <mdash /> the
        map that news outlets, governments, and researchers everywhere
        pulled numbers from. The underlying data was published openly, and
        it started out <term>wide</term>: one row per country or region, and
        one column for every date, holding that location's cumulative case
        count on that day.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-jhu-wide-problem">
      <title>A table with no final column</title>

      <p>
        Most wide tables have a fixed, known set of columns. This one did
        not. Every single day the pandemic continued, the dataset needed one
        more column than it had the day before, forever, for as long as
        anyone was tracking the outbreak. Anyone who wanted to do more than
        eyeball the day's totals <mdash /> plot a trend, compute a weekly
        change, merge in a country's population to calculate a rate <mdash />
        first had to reshape the table from wide to long, by hand, and had
        to do it again every time a new day's column was added. Wide format
        was not <em>hard</em> to use. It was hard to use <em>repeatedly, at
        growing scale, indefinitely.</em>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-jhu-switch">
      <title>The switch, and what it fixed</title>

      <p>
        In March 2020, JHU CSSE changed the structure of the time-series
        data. The new version moved away from one-column-per-date toward a
        structure built around standardised identifiers <mdash /> ISO
        3166-1 alpha-3 country codes <mdash /> specifically so that the
        COVID-19 figures could be merged automatically with other datasets,
        such as national population figures, rather than requiring a
        country-name lookup and a manual reshape first. Practitioners
        documented the change within hours of it happening: a blog post
        already partway through explaining how to tidy the old wide format
        had to be revised the same day the new format shipped.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-jhu-shapes-lesson">
        <title>The numbers didn't change. What you could do with them did.</title>
        <p>
          Nothing about the underlying case counts was different before and
          after this change. What changed was which operations were easy.
          In wide format, a human reader could scan across one country's row
          and watch the trend by eye <mdash /> genuinely useful for a
          dashboard glanced at once a day. But merging, filtering, grouping,
          and plotting all became easier once the same numbers were
          rearranged so that each row was a single observation: one country,
          one date, one case count. Shape did not change what was true. It
          changed what was <em>practical</em>, and at the scale this dataset
          reached, that difference determined whether the data could be used
          at all.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <p>
        <term>Sources:</term> Dong, E., Du, H. and Gardner, L. (2020).
        <q>An interactive web-based dashboard to track COVID-19 in real
        time.</q> <em>The Lancet Infectious Diseases</em>, 20(5), 533<ndash />534.
        Gassen, J. (2020). <q>Tidying the new Johns Hopkins Covid-19
        time-series datasets.</q> Blog post, 24 March 2020. Data repository:
        <url href="https://github.com/CSSEGISandData/COVID-19" visual="github.com/CSSEGISandData/COVID-19" />.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  SECTION 2: brief Ebola callback                                  -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <section xml:id="sec-shapes-ebola-callback">
    <title>A Shape Problem You've Already Met</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        <xref ref="ss-ebola-transmission" /> described something that,
        looked at again, is a shape problem. Sierra Leone's Ebola response
        did not fail to merge its district data because anyone lost it or
        typed it wrong. It failed because <q>each district response centre
        had adopted its own method for recording cases,</q> and inconsistent
        Excel line-list formats across districts <q>caused ongoing problems
        merging and aggregating cases into national totals.</q> Different
        districts were not disagreeing about what happened. They were
        recording the same kind of information in incompatible shapes, the
        same failure mode as the JHU dataset above, just without a single
        format anyone had agreed to reshape toward.
      </p>

      <p>
        The two cases make the same point from opposite directions. JHU
        CSSE controlled one format and changed it once, deliberately, and
        every user benefited immediately. Sierra Leone's districts each
        controlled their own format independently, with no shared standard
        to converge on, and the cost was paid every time anyone tried to
        combine them. Agreeing on a shape in advance is far cheaper than
        reconciling incompatible shapes after the fact.
      </p>
    </introduction>
  </section>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  SECTION 3: applied to the flamingo data                          -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <section xml:id="sec-shapes-applied">
    <title>Wide and Long, Applied</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        <c>kenya_flamingos.csv</c> has always been in <term>long</term>
        format: one row per bird, one column per variable. That shape is
        why every <c>ggplot2</c> call in
        <xref ref="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data" /> worked without
        any reshaping. But long format is not always what you want to
        <em>look</em> at, and reshaping between the two is a skill in its
        own right.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-shapes-long-to-wide">
      <title>From long to wide: making a report table</title>

      <p>
        Suppose you want a simple summary table, birds per lake per species,
        laid out the way a person reading a report would expect: lakes down
        the side, species across the top.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        library(dplyr)
        library(tidyr)

        counts_long &lt;- flamingos %&gt;%
          count(lake, species)

        head(counts_long)

        #&gt;          lake          species  n
        #&gt; 1     Bogoria Greater Flamingo 10
        #&gt; 2     Bogoria  Lesser Flamingo 114
        #&gt; 3 Elementaita Greater Flamingo 30
        #&gt; 4 Elementaita  Lesser Flamingo 38
        #&gt; 5      Nakuru Greater Flamingo 26
        #&gt; 6      Nakuru  Lesser Flamingo 126
      </program>

      <p>
        <c>counts_long</c> is exactly the shape a computer wants: one row
        per lake-species combination, one column holding the count. It is
        not the shape a human wants to read at a glance.
        <c>pivot_wider()</c> turns the <c>species</c> values into column
        names.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        counts_wide &lt;- counts_long %&gt;%
          pivot_wider(names_from = species, values_from = n)

        counts_wide

        #&gt;          lake Greater Flamingo Lesser Flamingo
        #&gt; 1     Bogoria               10             114
        #&gt; 2 Elementaita               30              38
        #&gt; 3      Nakuru               26             126
      </program>

      <p>
        Same numbers, same information, a different shape <mdash /> and this
        is <xref ref="table-lake-species-crosstab" /> from
        <xref ref="ch-exploring-data" />, arrived at mechanically rather than
        typed by hand.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-shapes-wide-to-long">
      <title>From wide to long: getting back to something plottable</title>

      <p>
        Reverse the process with <c>pivot_longer()</c>, and <c>counts_wide</c>
        becomes plottable again.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        counts_wide %&gt;%
          pivot_longer(cols = -lake, names_to = "species", values_to = "n")

        #&gt; # A tibble: 6 x 3
        #&gt;   lake        species              n
        #&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;       &lt;chr&gt;            &lt;int&gt;
        #&gt; 1 Bogoria     Greater Flamingo    10
        #&gt; 2 Bogoria     Lesser Flamingo    114
        #&gt; 3 Elementaita Greater Flamingo    30
        #&gt; 4 Elementaita Lesser Flamingo     38
        #&gt; 5 Nakuru      Greater Flamingo    26
        #&gt; 6 Nakuru      Lesser Flamingo    126
      </program>

      <p>
        This round trip, long <mdash /> wide <mdash /> long, is not busywork.
        Each direction serves a different reader. Long format serves R:
        <c>ggplot2</c>, <c>group_by()</c>, and joins all expect it. Wide
        format serves a person scanning a table by eye, or a spreadsheet
        cell reference that assumes species live in fixed columns. Knowing
        which one a task actually needs, before writing any code, saves
        exactly the repeated reshaping that cost the JHU CSSE team, and
        exactly the merging failure that cost Sierra Leone's Ebola response.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  OBJECTIVES                                                       -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <objectives xml:id="objectives-data-shapes">
    <title>By the end of this topic you will be able to</title>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Distinguish wide and long data shapes, and identify which one a given dataset is in.</p></li>
      <li><p>Explain why the same data, differently shaped, can make an operation trivial or nearly impossible.</p></li>
      <li><p>Reshape data between wide and long using <c>pivot_wider()</c> and <c>pivot_longer()</c>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Recognise a merging or aggregation failure as a shape problem, not just a data-quality problem.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  EXERCISES                                                        -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-data-shapes">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-shapes-jhu-daily">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="ss-jhu-wide-problem" /> says a new column had to be
          added to the wide-format dataset every single day. Sketch, in
          words, what would have gone wrong for a downstream user's code if
          it referenced date columns by a fixed position (for example,
          <q>column 47</q>) rather than by name. Would switching to long
          format on its own have fixed that particular problem? Why or why
          not?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-shapes-ebola-standard">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-shapes-ebola-callback" /> argues that agreeing on a
          shape in advance is cheaper than reconciling incompatible shapes
          afterward. Propose one concrete rule Sierra Leone's district
          response centres could have agreed to in advance <mdash /> about
          column names, row structure, or file format <mdash /> that would
          have prevented the merging problem described in
          <xref ref="ss-ebola-transmission" />.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-shapes-flamingo-wide">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using <xref ref="ss-shapes-long-to-wide" /> as a model, build a
          wide table of mean <c>body_mass_g</c> by <c>lake</c> (rows) and
          <c>species</c> (columns), instead of counts. Which version, this
          one or <xref ref="table-lake-species-crosstab" />, would you send
          to someone who wanted to read it in an email, and which would you
          hand to someone who wanted to plot it in <c>ggplot2</c>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-shapes-gapminder-wide">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The <c>gapminder</c> package from
          <xref ref="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data" /> is in long
          format: one row per country per year. Using <c>pivot_wider()</c>,
          build a table with one row per country and one column per year,
          holding <c>lifeExp</c>. Try plotting directly from this wide
          version with the same <c>ggplot2</c> code from
          <xref ref="ss-gapminder-2d" />. What breaks, and why?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>

</chapter>
